# Rank 1559 / CVE-2016-5426: PowerDNS's DNS name parser accepted qnames
# whose wire length exceeded the 255-byte protocol limit, so a crafted
# query with many labels (or a compression chain) drove oversized names
# into downstream handling (improper input validation, CWE-128/CWE-400
# family). getLabelFromContent in pdns/dnsparser.cc walked the packet's
# labels in a conditionless `for(;;)` whose compression-pointer branch
# recursed -- `return getLabelFromContent(content, offset, ret,
# ++recurs)` -- and the walk's only bounds were the depth counter
# (`recurs > 100`) and a cap on the assembled string checked *after*
# appending (`if (ret.length() > 1024) throw MOADNSException("Total
# name too long")`), both already present at the parent. Pool commit
# 881b5b03a5 is the real fix (COMMIT_OK -- "Reject qname's wirelength >
# 255, chopOff() handle dot inside labels"): a `size_t& wirelength`
# reference threads through the walk, incremented once per label and
# grown by `labellen`, with `if (wirelength + labellen > 255) throw`
# before each append; the compression branch decrements first because a
# pointer costs no wire bytes. chopOff/chopOffDotted in pdns/misc.cc
# additionally stop treating an escaped dot as a label separator. No
# pool correction.
#
# ## Re-measured at the current tip (span r1500-1599 recheck)
#
# Three `--cache off` runs per revision (timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB):
# the three files the fix touches report 48 findings each at both
# revisions -- 9 VYQL-MEM-016, 8 VYQL-MEM-006 plus VYQL-MEM-002 rows,
# every difference a line-number shift inside the two edited files --
# and a whole-tree scan of the checkout reports 2203 findings at both
# revisions with an identical (rule, file) multiset over three runs per
# side. The `-flags only` view is empty at both revisions on the whole
# tree, so this is a real zero and not a `-flags` replacement misread.
#
# ## Why the nearest shipped vocabulary does not reach it
#
# The one relevant later-shipped item is goUnboundedAppendAccumulation
# (bindings/go/unboundedAppendAccumulation.vyql, rank 1202's recheck),
# which reports a conditionless `for {}` loop growing a slice whose
# enclosing function never compares that slice's len or cap, through
# code.UnboundedAllocationReview / VYQL-DOS-041. Its frontend fact is
# Go-only, and porting its semantics to C++ verbatim -- a function
# context that grows a container through append/push_back/strcat and
# never consults its length or size -- was probed as a throwaway
# binding on this checkout: it reports 177 findings on the pdns tree,
# the same 177 at both revisions, and does not label
# getLabelFromContent at either. The reason is in the source above: the
# *vulnerable* walk already consults the accumulated name's own length
# inside the loop. The defect is not a missing budget but a wrong one --
# 1024 where RFC 1035 caps a wire name at 255 -- and the correct number
# is protocol knowledge no construct-level predicate carries.
#
# The only formulations that do separate the two revisions key on what
# the fix introduced. Counting any loop-grown variable as the budget
# suppresses both sides, because the parent's `recurs > 100` compares a
# variable the walk increments; excluding constant-grown counters so
# that only data-dependent growth (the fix's `wirelength += labellen`)
# counts as the budget does separate them, but that refinement is
# reverse-engineered from this diff -- the fix's own layout, not the
# weakness. Naming `wirelength` or the constant 255 names PowerDNS.
#
# Pin both revisions as a double miss. When protocol-budget vocabulary
# that can express "the decoder enforces the wire limit its own protocol
# documents" lands, the first block must flip to expect.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1559: the label walk bounds depth and a 1024 cap, not the wire budget"
  lang cpp
  file pdns/dnsparser.cc
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  #include <cstdint>
  #include <string>
  #include <vector>

  void getLabelFromContent(const std::vector<uint8_t>& content, uint16_t& frompos,
                           std::string& ret, int recurs)
  {
      if (recurs > 100)
          return;

      for (;;) {
          unsigned char labellen = content.at(frompos++);

          if (!labellen) {
              if (ret.empty())
                  ret.append(1, '.');
              break;
          }
          else if ((labellen & 0xc0) == 0xc0) {
              uint16_t offset = content.at(frompos++);
              return getLabelFromContent(content, offset, ret, ++recurs);
          }
          else if (labellen > 63) {
              return;
          }
          else {
              for (unsigned n = 0; n < labellen; ++n, frompos++)
                  ret.append(1, content[frompos]);
              ret.append(1, '.');
          }
          if (ret.length() > 1024)
              return;
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1559: the fixed revision charges every label byte against the wire budget"
  lang cpp
  file pdns/dnsparser.cc
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  #include <cstddef>
  #include <cstdint>
  #include <string>
  #include <vector>

  void getLabelFromContent(const std::vector<uint8_t>& content, uint16_t& frompos,
                           std::string& ret, int recurs, size_t& wirelength)
  {
      if (recurs > 100)
          return;

      for (;;) {
          unsigned char labellen = content.at(frompos++);

          wirelength++;
          if (wirelength > 255)
              return;

          if (!labellen) {
              if (ret.empty())
                  ret.append(1, '.');
              break;
          }
          else if ((labellen & 0xc0) == 0xc0) {
              uint16_t offset = content.at(frompos++);
              /* the compression pointer does not count into the wire length */
              return getLabelFromContent(content, offset, ret, ++recurs, --wirelength);
          }
          else if (labellen > 63) {
              return;
          }
          else {
              if (wirelength + labellen > 255)
                  return;
              wirelength += labellen;
              for (unsigned n = 0; n < labellen; ++n, frompos++)
                  ret.append(1, content[frompos]);
              ret.append(1, '.');
          }
          if (ret.length() > 1024)
              return;
      }
  }
  ```
